§§ `Decimal Places Lookup`

GIVEN currencyCode IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING NUMBER
@nlg the number of decimal places of %currencyCode%
`decimal places for currency` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `US Dollar code` THEN RIGHT `US Dollar decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Euro code` THEN RIGHT `Euro decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `British Pound code` THEN RIGHT `British Pound decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Japanese Yen code` THEN RIGHT `Japanese Yen decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Swiss Franc code` THEN RIGHT `Swiss Franc decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Canadian Dollar code` THEN RIGHT `Canadian Dollar decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Australian Dollar code` THEN RIGHT `Australian Dollar decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Chinese Yuan code` THEN RIGHT `Chinese Yuan decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Hong Kong Dollar code` THEN RIGHT `Hong Kong Dollar decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Singapore Dollar code` THEN RIGHT `Singapore Dollar decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Indian Rupee code` THEN RIGHT `Indian Rupee decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Brazilian Real code` THEN RIGHT `Brazilian Real decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Mexican Peso code` THEN RIGHT `Mexican Peso decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `Swedish Krona code` THEN RIGHT `Swedish Krona decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `New Zealand Dollar code` THEN RIGHT `New Zealand Dollar decimals`
        IF currencyCode EQUALS `South Korean Won code` THEN RIGHT `South Korean Won decimals`
        OTHERWISE LEFT (CONCAT "Unknown currency code: ", currencyCode)


§§ `Power of 10`

GIVEN exponent IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
@nlg 10 to the power of %exponent% if that is 0, 1, 2 or 3, otherwise 100
`power of 10` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF exponent EQUALS 0 THEN 1
        IF exponent EQUALS 1 THEN 10
        IF exponent EQUALS 2 THEN 100
        IF exponent EQUALS 3 THEN 1000
        OTHERWISE 100  -- Default to 2 decimals


§§ `Currency Amount Constructor`

-- Creates a monetary amount from integer minor units (cents)
-- Example: Money 12345 "USD" represents $123.45

GIVEN minorUnits IS A NUMBER
      currencyCode IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING STRING
@nlg the written amount of %minorUnits% minor units of %currencyCode%
`Money` MEANS
    CONSIDER `decimal places for currency` currencyCode
    WHEN LEFT err THEN LEFT err
    WHEN RIGHT decimals THEN
        BRANCH
            IF NOT minorUnits EQUALS FLOOR minorUnits
            THEN LEFT "Minor units must be an integer"
            OTHERWISE RIGHT (formatAmount minorUnits decimals currencyCode)
    WHERE
        formatAmount units decs code MEANS
            CONCAT
                CONCAT (formatNumber units decs), " "
                , code

        formatNumber units decs MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF decs EQUALS 0
                THEN numberToString units
                OTHERWISE
                    CONCAT
                        CONCAT (numberToString majorUnits), "."
                        , paddedMinor
                    WHERE
                    divisor MEANS `power of 10` decs
                        majorUnits MEANS FLOOR (units DIVIDED BY divisor)
                        minorPart MEANS units MODULO divisor
                        paddedMinor MEANS padLeft (numberToString minorPart) decs "0"

        numberToString n MEANS TOSTRING n

        padLeft str targetLen padChar MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF STRINGLENGTH str AT LEAST targetLen
                THEN str
                OTHERWISE padLeft (CONCAT padChar, str) targetLen padChar


§§ `Amount Conversion Functions`

-- Convert from major units (dollars) to minor units (cents)
GIVEN majorUnits IS A NUMBER
      currencyCode IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING NUMBER
@nlg the minor units in %majorUnits% major units of %currencyCode%, rounded down
`major to minor units` MEANS
    CONSIDER `decimal places for currency` currencyCode
    WHEN LEFT err THEN LEFT err
    WHEN RIGHT decimals THEN
        RIGHT (FLOOR (majorUnits TIMES (`power of 10` decimals)))

-- Convert from minor units (cents) to major units (dollars)
GIVEN minorUnits IS A NUMBER
      currencyCode IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING NUMBER
@nlg the major units in %minorUnits% minor units of %currencyCode%
`minor to major units` MEANS
    CONSIDER `decimal places for currency` currencyCode
    WHEN LEFT err THEN LEFT err
    WHEN RIGHT decimals THEN
        RIGHT (minorUnits DIVIDED BY (`power of 10` decimals))


§§ `Arithmetic Operations on Money`

-- Add two amounts in the same currency
GIVEN amount1 IS A NUMBER
      amount2 IS A NUMBER
      currencyCode IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING NUMBER
@nlg the sum of %amount1% and %amount2% in %currencyCode%
`add money` MEANS
    CONSIDER `decimal places for currency` currencyCode
    WHEN LEFT err THEN LEFT err
    WHEN RIGHT decimals THEN RIGHT (amount1 PLUS amount2)

-- Subtract two amounts in the same currency
GIVEN amount1 IS A NUMBER
      amount2 IS A NUMBER
      currencyCode IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING NUMBER
@nlg %amount1% less %amount2% in %currencyCode%
`subtract money` MEANS
    CONSIDER `decimal places for currency` currencyCode
    WHEN LEFT err THEN LEFT err
    WHEN RIGHT decimals THEN RIGHT (amount1 MINUS amount2)

-- Multiply amount by a factor (for percentages, etc.)
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
      factor IS A NUMBER
      currencyCode IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING NUMBER
@nlg %amount% multiplied by %factor% in %currencyCode%, rounded down
`multiply money` MEANS
    CONSIDER `decimal places for currency` currencyCode
    WHEN LEFT err THEN LEFT err
    WHEN RIGHT decimals THEN
        RIGHT (FLOOR (amount TIMES factor))

-- Divide amount by a divisor
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
      divisor IS A NUMBER
      currencyCode IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING NUMBER
@nlg %amount% divided by %divisor% in %currencyCode%, rounded down
`divide money` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF divisor EQUALS 0
        THEN LEFT "Cannot divide by zero"
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `decimal places for currency` currencyCode
            WHEN LEFT err THEN LEFT err
            WHEN RIGHT decimals THEN
                RIGHT (FLOOR (amount DIVIDED BY divisor))


§§ `Comparison Operations`

GIVEN amount1 IS A NUMBER
      amount2 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg %amount1% equals %amount2%
`money equal` MEANS
    amount1 EQUALS amount2

GIVEN amount1 IS A NUMBER
      amount2 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg %amount1% is more than %amount2%
`money greater than` MEANS
    amount1 GREATER THAN amount2

GIVEN amount1 IS A NUMBER
      amount2 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg %amount1% is less than %amount2%
`money less than` MEANS
    amount1 LESS THAN amount2

GIVEN amount1 IS A NUMBER
      amount2 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg %amount1% is at least %amount2%
`money at least` MEANS
    amount1 AT LEAST amount2

GIVEN amount1 IS A NUMBER
      amount2 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg %amount1% is at most %amount2%
`money at most` MEANS
    amount1 AT MOST amount2


§§ `Validation Functions`

GIVEN code IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg %code% has three characters and no lower-case letters, like an ISO 4217 code
`is valid currency code` MEANS
    STRINGLENGTH code EQUALS 3
    AND code EQUALS TOUPPER code

GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg %amount% is zero or more
`is non-negative` MEANS
    amount AT LEAST 0

GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg %amount% is more than zero
`is positive` MEANS
    amount GREATER THAN 0
